# Experiment 3: loopback listener (Rust std::net) vs TSX net (nsh 0.4.0 e626a8d)
Date: 2026-11-07 (UTC)

## A. Rust: bind 127.0.0.1 with std::net only — macOS 27.2 (arm64)
Probe: /Users/hmziq/fo/nsh-auth/.zflow/scratch/loopback/loopback.rs (compiled with rustc, no dependencies).
Full transcript of the run:

    listening on 127.0.0.1:54166
    simulated redirect: http://127.0.0.1:54166/oauth2redirect?code=test-code&state=abc
    accepted from 127.0.0.1:54167
    request line: GET /oauth2redirect?code=test-code&state=abc HTTP/1.1
    query has code: true
    query has state: true
    client got response: true
    OK
    (exit 0)

IPv6 and hostname probe (same machine):

    [::1] bound on port 54202
    localhost bound: Ok([::1]:54203) (resolves to Ok(["[::1]:0", "127.0.0.1:0"]))

Conclusion: a Rust crate binds 127.0.0.1 and [::1] on a random port with
std::net::TcpListener, serves the redirect request and answers the browser.
Implementation note found by the first (hung) attempt: read to the end of
headers, not to EOF — the client socket stays open under HTTP semantics and
read_to_string blocks forever. Also "localhost" resolves to [::1] first here,
which is why RFC 8252 and Google both say use the IP literal 127.0.0.1.

## B. TSX: net module is outbound only
Source of truth: .nsh/types/net.d.ts (generated by nsh 0.4.0 in .zflow/scratch/research-app).
Full export surface: `ConnectOptions { host, port }`, `connect(options): Promise<Socket>`,
`class Socket { read(); write(); close(); dispose(); }`, and a default export with `connect`.
Searched: `grep -i "listen|server|bind" .nsh/types/net.d.ts` -> no hits.
The connect doc says: "Connects to { host, port }, only to pairs granted by
capabilities.network.sockets (ERR_ACCESS_DENIED ...), at most 32 sockets per VM".
No `listen`, `bind`, `Server` or `createServer` exists anywhere in .nsh/types
(grep over the whole directory).
Conclusion: TSX can dial out (e.g. to the mock broker) but cannot host the
RFC 8252 loopback redirect. The loopback listener must live in Rust native code
(not sandboxed, see tooling.md: "A Rust crate's own native code, such as std::fs,
is not sandboxed; it has the app's trust") or in an embedding host.
